WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.170
Brace yourselves, we're about to get into
some serious detail about telephone systems.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:08.930
I'm at the Museum of Communications in Seattle,
to answer a question:

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:12.519
in old Hollywood films, when someone is on
a phone call and they got hung up on,

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.320
why did they hear a dial tone?

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.360
[dial tone]

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:23.240
"Well, you can stick your well-laid plan up
your well-laid ass." [dial tone]

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:27.700
Supervision is the word that describes how
a telephone switch

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:32.880
knows whether the calling and called parties
are on hook or off hook.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.960
When you end a call and hang up,

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.480
the equipment receives that on-hook signal,

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.440
and anything that was in service at that time

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.180
disconnects and goes back to its normal state.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:44.870
So you end up with dead air.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.870
Now, the reason that a sound director would
use a dial tone

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:50.040
instead of just nothing is kind of obvious,
right?

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.750
It's to make it clear that the other side
has hung up.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.910
That's certainly what lots of people argue
online.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:58.559
Except they could just do that by using one
short sound effect.

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:00.200
And sometimes, they do.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.090
"Don't ever call me again."

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:03.100
[rattling click]

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.100
"Wow!

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.100
I...

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.100
I guess you're home."

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:07.100
"Good luck."

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.400
[rattling click]

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.510
It turns out there's another reason.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:13.750
Back in the days of celluloid film,

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.310
southern California was one of the few places
in America

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.030
with independent telephone companies,

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:23.380
not part of the giant Bell monopoly that handled
the rest of the country.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.600
Well, behind us is a step-by-step system

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.800
and it's a relative of the telephone systems

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.030
that were used in most of southern California.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.180
The Bell system didn't have as much of a monopoly
there.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:40.500
As I dial a number, various elements of the
step connect me

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.180
through the system from start to finish.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.360
And that's where it got the name step-by-step.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.979
As I finish dialling, the call will connect...

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.140
[ringing]

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.680
...and it can be answered.

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:59.200
Now, in the step system, it doesn't have what's
called 'far-end supervision'.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.829
And that means that the called party can hang
up and pick up again

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:07.109
as many times as they want, and the call won't
disconnect.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.840
But if the calling party hangs up...

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.940
[dial tone]

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.640
...the called party will get hit with dial
tone right away

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.930
because the system doesn't know whether I've
been hung up on

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.280
or whether I've just picked up the phone.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.000
So in Hollywood, where all of the movies were
being written and filmed,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.840
their experience on the telephone was actually
different

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.500
from most of the rest of America, and the
world.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.780
Which, let's be honest, is kind of like so
many other things in Hollywood.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.080
The bottom line is:

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.460
the folks making the movies really did get
a dial tone when they were hung up on,

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.209
at least sometimes, so they put that in their
films.

00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:50.770
Or they picked whichever option worked best
for the scene.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:54.280
If it's still happening when a character uses
a cell phone, though?

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:55.280
That's just lazy.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.290
Thank you to all the team at Seattle's wonderful
Museum of Communications!

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.599
You can check out their YouTube channel over
here

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:03.849
or pull down the description for a link to
the Museum and to see their opening hours

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:07.219
and a list of all the incredible equipment
they've got here.

